[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [[:db/retract 17592186382187 :answer-field/choices 17592186382192] [:db/retract 17592186382187 :answer-field/choices 17592186382189] [:db/retract 17592186382187 :answer-field/choices 17592186382190] [:db/retract 17592186382187 :answer-field/choices 17592186382191] [:db/retract 17592186382201 :answer-field/choices 17592186382202] [:db/retract 17592186382201 :answer-field/choices 17592186382203] [:db/retract 17592186382201 :answer-field/choices 17592186382204] [:db/retract 17592186382201 :answer-field/choices 17592186382205] [:db/retract 17592186382201 :answer-field/choices 17592186382206] [:db/retract 17592186338583 :answer-field/choices 17592186336081] [:db/retract 17592186338583 :answer-field/choices 17592186337405] [:db/retract 17592186338583 :answer-field/choices 17592186337387] [:db/retract 17592186338583 :answer-field/choices 17592186336460] [:db/retract 17592186338583 :answer-field/choices 17592186336445] [:db/retract 17592186338720 :answer-field/choices 17592186338218] [:db/retract 17592186338720 :answer-field/choices 17592186336612] [:db/retract 17592186338720 :answer-field/choices 17592186336871] [:db/retract 17592186338720 :answer-field/choices 17592186336089] [:db/retract 17592186338720 :answer-field/choices 17592186337482] [:db/retract 17592186338315 :answer-field/choices 17592186337921] [:db/retract 17592186338315 :answer-field/choices 17592186337875] [:db/retract 17592186338315 :answer-field/choices 17592186337575] [:db/retract 17592186338315 :answer-field/choices 17592186337292] [:db/retract 17592186338315 :answer-field/choices 17592186337566] [:db/retract 17592186338495 :answer-field/choices 17592186337698] [:db/retract 17592186338495 :answer-field/choices 17592186337476] [:db/retract 17592186338495 :answer-field/choices 17592186337416] [:db/retract 17592186338495 :answer-field/choices 17592186337337] [:db/retract 17592186338495 :answer-field/choices 17592186336555] {:db/id 17592186124465 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6a7e0438-dfaf-58ac-9ade-c318de994348" "question-93b5347d-fa82-5e5e-b9aa-42f525c17d43"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124557 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-88858de7-2fd7-5431-aec4-cee489f567e8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1ddf04e4-d463-5268-9d26-58b4ea7da263" "question-73a5b476-e534-54e3-9da7-2de0545b138d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186382186 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & \\ge 0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y\\ge 0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x+y}}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}-\\sqrt{x+y}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y-2x-2y}{2{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x+y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2x-y}{2{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x+y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y\\ge 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186382187 :answer-field/correct "answer-b0492aba-dbff-56e3-abcb-5c0c52db1196" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7be4488a-cd3f-50bb-9afc-fa094c75e607" "answer-b0492aba-dbff-56e3-abcb-5c0c52db1196" "answer-3a8771b6-55f7-537e-b0a2-1396634fe7c3" "answer-18320f2a-b614-5878-a30c-a6927276e203"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7be4488a-cd3f-50bb-9afc-fa094c75e607" :answer/id #uuid "7be4488a-cd3f-50bb-9afc-fa094c75e607" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x+y>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0492aba-dbff-56e3-abcb-5c0c52db1196" :answer/id #uuid "b0492aba-dbff-56e3-abcb-5c0c52db1196" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a8771b6-55f7-537e-b0a2-1396634fe7c3" :answer/id #uuid "3a8771b6-55f7-537e-b0a2-1396634fe7c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y\\ge 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18320f2a-b614-5878-a30c-a6927276e203" :answer/id #uuid "18320f2a-b614-5878-a30c-a6927276e203" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6a7e0438-dfaf-58ac-9ade-c318de994348" :question/id #uuid "6a7e0438-dfaf-58ac-9ade-c318de994348" :question/math-academy-id "q-132637" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{x}{y+z},$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when $y+z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y+z}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{(y+z)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+z)^{2} & \\ne 0 \\\\ y+z & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-115a4c5e-8438-57e0-8218-a23a116369b2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-115a4c5e-8438-57e0-8218-a23a116369b2" :answer-field/id #uuid "115a4c5e-8438-57e0-8218-a23a116369b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-93c1ef46-0b11-5266-8f41-56facaa40905" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bdb1c6fb-2082-5cdc-b6d2-731c9c2a61b8" "answer-365f8bbd-ce03-569d-aa3f-4ca177bcd8ce" "answer-7240289b-9821-502d-a55b-8731f37e7d59" "answer-3d2420ba-4165-5c8f-9927-4bbc2b816817" "answer-93c1ef46-0b11-5266-8f41-56facaa40905"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bdb1c6fb-2082-5cdc-b6d2-731c9c2a61b8" :answer/id #uuid "bdb1c6fb-2082-5cdc-b6d2-731c9c2a61b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z<0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-365f8bbd-ce03-569d-aa3f-4ca177bcd8ce" :answer/id #uuid "365f8bbd-ce03-569d-aa3f-4ca177bcd8ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z>0\\,\\text{and}\\,x\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7240289b-9821-502d-a55b-8731f37e7d59" :answer/id #uuid "7240289b-9821-502d-a55b-8731f37e7d59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d2420ba-4165-5c8f-9927-4bbc2b816817" :answer/id #uuid "3d2420ba-4165-5c8f-9927-4bbc2b816817" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93c1ef46-0b11-5266-8f41-56facaa40905" :answer/id #uuid "93c1ef46-0b11-5266-8f41-56facaa40905" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-93b5347d-fa82-5e5e-b9aa-42f525c17d43" :question/id #uuid "93b5347d-fa82-5e5e-b9aa-42f525c17d43" :question/math-academy-id "q-36525" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-{x}^{2}}},$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ exists." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ y & >{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-{x}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{-2x}{2(y-{x}^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{(y-{x}^{2})^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ y & >{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d731db7f-90f6-50ca-af14-f65d325828c8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d731db7f-90f6-50ca-af14-f65d325828c8" :answer-field/id #uuid "d731db7f-90f6-50ca-af14-f65d325828c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-14b28e62-5e5c-5fa4-828d-8176c468f073" :answer-field/choices ["answer-14b28e62-5e5c-5fa4-828d-8176c468f073" "answer-f1bbb24c-f728-542a-b6fd-83644bef18b4" "answer-94fa791e-d489-52b8-9318-5172cd32b632" "answer-f3c96cca-e498-5185-8483-99a97501e744" "answer-ac2ab564-0469-5d5f-bd5b-92ae71f9cfcb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14b28e62-5e5c-5fa4-828d-8176c468f073" :answer/id #uuid "14b28e62-5e5c-5fa4-828d-8176c468f073" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y):y>{x}^{2}\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1bbb24c-f728-542a-b6fd-83644bef18b4" :answer/id #uuid "f1bbb24c-f728-542a-b6fd-83644bef18b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y<{x}^{2}\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94fa791e-d489-52b8-9318-5172cd32b632" :answer/id #uuid "94fa791e-d489-52b8-9318-5172cd32b632" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ge {x}^{2}\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3c96cca-e498-5185-8483-99a97501e744" :answer/id #uuid "f3c96cca-e498-5185-8483-99a97501e744" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\le {x}^{2}\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac2ab564-0469-5d5f-bd5b-92ae71f9cfcb" :answer/id #uuid "ac2ab564-0469-5d5f-bd5b-92ae71f9cfcb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${f}_{x}$ exists everywhere on the $xy$-plane" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186382200 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined when $-1\\le xy\\le 1.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le xy\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(xy)^{2}}}\\cdot x \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}{y}^{2} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{2} & <1 \\\\ -1<xy & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le xy\\le 1\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186382201 :answer-field/correct "answer-e48eb178-d3ae-5b7a-9e32-6647d4f89008" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e48eb178-d3ae-5b7a-9e32-6647d4f89008" "answer-564c0717-2ab8-59c0-a054-e88b4b250ec9" "answer-6e63fc50-7cc5-5095-bd95-f14db7b98d09" "answer-79221a8e-d0bd-56c1-92e8-362d0c95c652" "answer-856a2403-496c-5b21-9944-c9e1b1c13646"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e48eb178-d3ae-5b7a-9e32-6647d4f89008" :answer/id #uuid "e48eb178-d3ae-5b7a-9e32-6647d4f89008" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-564c0717-2ab8-59c0-a054-e88b4b250ec9" :answer/id #uuid "564c0717-2ab8-59c0-a054-e88b4b250ec9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le xy\\le 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e63fc50-7cc5-5095-bd95-f14db7b98d09" :answer/id #uuid "6e63fc50-7cc5-5095-bd95-f14db7b98d09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy<1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79221a8e-d0bd-56c1-92e8-362d0c95c652" :answer/id #uuid "79221a8e-d0bd-56c1-92e8-362d0c95c652" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-856a2403-496c-5b21-9944-c9e1b1c13646" :answer/id #uuid "856a2403-496c-5b21-9944-c9e1b1c13646" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy\\ne 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186339046 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y+z)$ is defined for any $x$, $y$, and $z.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}=\\frac{1}{1+(x+y+z)^{2}}$ is defined for any $x$, $y$, and $z.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domains of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186338583 :answer-field/correct "answer-56dbc98e-986f-5c73-aa5f-cac881ecfa04" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d0ae369d-fbce-5a92-b83c-fdb84536b23d" "answer-c7f3adcf-6cdf-592b-b898-6f8526012b19" "answer-cdbf817f-a650-51de-829b-812fa24d59e2" "answer-42768df1-45c5-5a14-8236-adb8b26820e4" "answer-56dbc98e-986f-5c73-aa5f-cac881ecfa04"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0ae369d-fbce-5a92-b83c-fdb84536b23d" :answer/id #uuid "d0ae369d-fbce-5a92-b83c-fdb84536b23d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x+y+z)^{2}>1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7f3adcf-6cdf-592b-b898-6f8526012b19" :answer/id #uuid "c7f3adcf-6cdf-592b-b898-6f8526012b19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>-1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cdbf817f-a650-51de-829b-812fa24d59e2" :answer/id #uuid "cdbf817f-a650-51de-829b-812fa24d59e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42768df1-45c5-5a14-8236-adb8b26820e4" :answer/id #uuid "42768df1-45c5-5a14-8236-adb8b26820e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56dbc98e-986f-5c73-aa5f-cac881ecfa04" :answer/id #uuid "56dbc98e-986f-5c73-aa5f-cac881ecfa04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-88858de7-2fd7-5431-aec4-cee489f567e8" :question/id #uuid "88858de7-2fd7-5431-aec4-cee489f567e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-72028" :question/problem "Given that $h(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{xz}{{y}^{2}})$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y}=-\\frac{2}{y},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $h$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $h(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{xz}{{y}^{2}})$ is defined only when $xz>0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $h$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{h}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${h}_{x}=\\frac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${h}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${h}_{y}=-\\frac{2}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${h}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${h}_{z}=\\frac{1}{z}$ is defined only when $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${h}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $h$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{h}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{z}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\cap \\,\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-13e96fd7-067f-5c7e-8c81-eec0b11e5b02"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-13e96fd7-067f-5c7e-8c81-eec0b11e5b02" :answer-field/id #uuid "13e96fd7-067f-5c7e-8c81-eec0b11e5b02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c9035321-67de-5d87-8802-b231710205c2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c9035321-67de-5d87-8802-b231710205c2" "answer-6ae0216e-3dda-5b86-bf9a-7a2f1941c30d" "answer-928531e1-339e-5d41-9d09-292c01dfe676" "answer-a464505c-7c18-5824-af43-db6d78d3830b" "answer-2ec0f707-e54c-51dd-acbe-ce56f922c04b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9035321-67de-5d87-8802-b231710205c2" :answer/id #uuid "c9035321-67de-5d87-8802-b231710205c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ae0216e-3dda-5b86-bf9a-7a2f1941c30d" :answer/id #uuid "6ae0216e-3dda-5b86-bf9a-7a2f1941c30d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz\\ge 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-928531e1-339e-5d41-9d09-292c01dfe676" :answer/id #uuid "928531e1-339e-5d41-9d09-292c01dfe676" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz\\le 0\\,\\text{and}\\,y>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a464505c-7c18-5824-af43-db6d78d3830b" :answer/id #uuid "a464505c-7c18-5824-af43-db6d78d3830b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ec0f707-e54c-51dd-acbe-ce56f922c04b" :answer/id #uuid "2ec0f707-e54c-51dd-acbe-ce56f922c04b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186339047 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{1+y-{x}^{2}}}$ is defined only when $1+y-{x}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}=\\frac{xz}{\\sqrt{(1+y-{x}^{2})^{3}}}$ is defined only when $1+y-{x}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are the same\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186338720 :answer-field/correct "answer-58af975c-c2a1-526c-8913-b54cae912604" :answer-field/choices ["answer-58af975c-c2a1-526c-8913-b54cae912604" "answer-8325a7fb-2d1d-57b2-90c3-eb44f3fb67db" "answer-41ff0dd7-d72c-5277-8d4d-17134cb43aa4" "answer-fcadf2f9-91d3-553d-9019-a4aa4c81b373" "answer-6c293847-d86d-5648-bc3a-b4c65524ca2d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58af975c-c2a1-526c-8913-b54cae912604" :answer/id #uuid "58af975c-c2a1-526c-8913-b54cae912604" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8325a7fb-2d1d-57b2-90c3-eb44f3fb67db" :answer/id #uuid "8325a7fb-2d1d-57b2-90c3-eb44f3fb67db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}>y+1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41ff0dd7-d72c-5277-8d4d-17134cb43aa4" :answer/id #uuid "41ff0dd7-d72c-5277-8d4d-17134cb43aa4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\ge y+1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fcadf2f9-91d3-553d-9019-a4aa4c81b373" :answer/id #uuid "fcadf2f9-91d3-553d-9019-a4aa4c81b373" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y+1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c293847-d86d-5648-bc3a-b4c65524ca2d" :answer/id #uuid "6c293847-d86d-5648-bc3a-b4c65524ca2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\ne y+1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186339043 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $g(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186338315 :answer-field/correct "answer-3d4cb978-08f2-56c9-a2ea-886a3b926325" :answer-field/choices ["answer-783fd4c1-fb89-5517-bc35-b883098e9f40" "answer-6e834be1-c403-5b49-87e3-229d8e48f719" "answer-3d4cb978-08f2-56c9-a2ea-886a3b926325" "answer-e4c394bb-40ee-5d34-8bd0-d21a98e21ea0" "answer-00e7e3fe-8aa5-5dbe-b351-f8ab041da26a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-783fd4c1-fb89-5517-bc35-b883098e9f40" :answer/id #uuid "783fd4c1-fb89-5517-bc35-b883098e9f40" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e834be1-c403-5b49-87e3-229d8e48f719" :answer/id #uuid "6e834be1-c403-5b49-87e3-229d8e48f719" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d4cb978-08f2-56c9-a2ea-886a3b926325" :answer/id #uuid "3d4cb978-08f2-56c9-a2ea-886a3b926325" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4c394bb-40ee-5d34-8bd0-d21a98e21ea0" :answer/id #uuid "e4c394bb-40ee-5d34-8bd0-d21a98e21ea0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00e7e3fe-8aa5-5dbe-b351-f8ab041da26a" :answer/id #uuid "00e7e3fe-8aa5-5dbe-b351-f8ab041da26a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186339044 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}\\,{e}^{x/y}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{(2x+y){e}^{x/y}}{2y\\sqrt{x}}$ is defined only when $x>0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{y}(x,y)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}{e}^{x/y}}{{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186338495 :answer-field/correct "answer-68c920e8-acc6-5f92-901b-f110118b4e1e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-902fe96c-5b07-5372-abe9-ae12860196a2" "answer-5a32d06b-cf7c-5422-99a6-39dd7a840412" "answer-68c920e8-acc6-5f92-901b-f110118b4e1e" "answer-92d41990-0f98-5af9-a312-0df1d3c12535" "answer-b712b312-6703-550f-a665-341165a0b1ea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-902fe96c-5b07-5372-abe9-ae12860196a2" :answer/id #uuid "902fe96c-5b07-5372-abe9-ae12860196a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x=0,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a32d06b-cf7c-5422-99a6-39dd7a840412" :answer/id #uuid "5a32d06b-cf7c-5422-99a6-39dd7a840412" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68c920e8-acc6-5f92-901b-f110118b4e1e" :answer/id #uuid "68c920e8-acc6-5f92-901b-f110118b4e1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92d41990-0f98-5af9-a312-0df1d3c12535" :answer/id #uuid "92d41990-0f98-5af9-a312-0df1d3c12535" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y>0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b712b312-6703-550f-a665-341165a0b1ea" :answer/id #uuid "b712b312-6703-550f-a665-341165a0b1ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1ddf04e4-d463-5268-9d26-58b4ea7da263" :question/id #uuid "1ddf04e4-d463-5268-9d26-58b4ea7da263" :question/math-academy-id "q-72015" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)?$\n\nI. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(0,-1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{y}(1,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-y}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-y}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,-1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0-(-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)=0,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(0,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,-1) & =\\frac{1}{0-(-1)}=\\frac{1}{1}=1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,-1) & =-\\frac{1}{0-(-1)}=-\\frac{1}{1}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0-1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0).\n$$\n\nwhich is also undefined. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7baa4b68-d188-53a1-8a4e-8080f4629446"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7baa4b68-d188-53a1-8a4e-8080f4629446" :answer-field/id #uuid "7baa4b68-d188-53a1-8a4e-8080f4629446" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1f484384-2944-5aa5-be6d-f85e77de604a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e93c9a34-c0f4-5a44-8207-573fd788bf6b" "answer-1f484384-2944-5aa5-be6d-f85e77de604a" "answer-895a6aba-7fbc-5c6f-b46f-27ec0e197bc2" "answer-95fe521c-d73c-5a83-b40d-7b1ab28589f0" "answer-8b5eecf6-bced-54a8-8975-13e55e4eb747"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e93c9a34-c0f4-5a44-8207-573fd788bf6b" :answer/id #uuid "e93c9a34-c0f4-5a44-8207-573fd788bf6b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f484384-2944-5aa5-be6d-f85e77de604a" :answer/id #uuid "1f484384-2944-5aa5-be6d-f85e77de604a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-895a6aba-7fbc-5c6f-b46f-27ec0e197bc2" :answer/id #uuid "895a6aba-7fbc-5c6f-b46f-27ec0e197bc2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95fe521c-d73c-5a83-b40d-7b1ab28589f0" :answer/id #uuid "95fe521c-d73c-5a83-b40d-7b1ab28589f0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b5eecf6-bced-54a8-8975-13e55e4eb747" :answer/id #uuid "8b5eecf6-bced-54a8-8975-13e55e4eb747" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-73a5b476-e534-54e3-9da7-2de0545b138d" :question/id #uuid "73a5b476-e534-54e3-9da7-2de0545b138d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72017" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}?$\n\nI. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(2,-1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(1,0)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(2,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(2,-1)=\\frac{2}{(-1)}=-2,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,-1) & =\\frac{1}{-1}=-1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(2,-1) & =-\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\frac{1}{0}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is true. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\frac{0}{1}=0,\n$$\n\n  and  ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{y}(0,1)=\\frac{-0}{{1}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-68e03a66-dcc9-5dc9-9fd3-bdca1b4cd4cd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-68e03a66-dcc9-5dc9-9fd3-bdca1b4cd4cd" :answer-field/id #uuid "68e03a66-dcc9-5dc9-9fd3-bdca1b4cd4cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-edfadd89-b8d6-5bb0-9938-41414b42c7a0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-edfadd89-b8d6-5bb0-9938-41414b42c7a0" "answer-2641419a-4016-5b04-ae0d-2222660b4187" "answer-198bc42c-d3a2-5588-8d98-878c5a5390fe" "answer-576eba6e-2ba8-56c8-a2cb-7dfa5b572111" "answer-51e3c066-4569-5d03-9a91-b2ea8579daae"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-edfadd89-b8d6-5bb0-9938-41414b42c7a0" :answer/id #uuid "edfadd89-b8d6-5bb0-9938-41414b42c7a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2641419a-4016-5b04-ae0d-2222660b4187" :answer/id #uuid "2641419a-4016-5b04-ae0d-2222660b4187" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-198bc42c-d3a2-5588-8d98-878c5a5390fe" :answer/id #uuid "198bc42c-d3a2-5588-8d98-878c5a5390fe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-576eba6e-2ba8-56c8-a2cb-7dfa5b572111" :answer/id #uuid "576eba6e-2ba8-56c8-a2cb-7dfa5b572111" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51e3c066-4569-5d03-9a91-b2ea8579daae" :answer/id #uuid "51e3c066-4569-5d03-9a91-b2ea8579daae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]}] "assertions" [[17592186382186 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & \\ge 0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y\\ge 0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x+y}}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}-\\sqrt{x+y}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y-2x-2y}{2{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x+y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2x-y}{2{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x+y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y\\ge 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["answer-7be4488a-cd3f-50bb-9afc-fa094c75e607" :answer/id #uuid "7be4488a-cd3f-50bb-9afc-fa094c75e607"] ["answer-7be4488a-cd3f-50bb-9afc-fa094c75e607" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-7be4488a-cd3f-50bb-9afc-fa094c75e607" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x+y>0\\}"] ["answer-b0492aba-dbff-56e3-abcb-5c0c52db1196" :answer/id #uuid "b0492aba-dbff-56e3-abcb-5c0c52db1196"] ["answer-b0492aba-dbff-56e3-abcb-5c0c52db1196" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-b0492aba-dbff-56e3-abcb-5c0c52db1196" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0\\}"] ["answer-3a8771b6-55f7-537e-b0a2-1396634fe7c3" :answer/id #uuid "3a8771b6-55f7-537e-b0a2-1396634fe7c3"] ["answer-3a8771b6-55f7-537e-b0a2-1396634fe7c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-3a8771b6-55f7-537e-b0a2-1396634fe7c3" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y\\ge 0\\}"] ["answer-18320f2a-b614-5878-a30c-a6927276e203" :answer/id #uuid "18320f2a-b614-5878-a30c-a6927276e203"] ["answer-18320f2a-b614-5878-a30c-a6927276e203" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-18320f2a-b614-5878-a30c-a6927276e203" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x\\ne 0\\}"] [17592186382187 :answer-field/correct "answer-b0492aba-dbff-56e3-abcb-5c0c52db1196"] [17592186382187 :answer-field/choices "answer-7be4488a-cd3f-50bb-9afc-fa094c75e607"] [17592186382187 :answer-field/choices "answer-b0492aba-dbff-56e3-abcb-5c0c52db1196"] [17592186382187 :answer-field/choices "answer-3a8771b6-55f7-537e-b0a2-1396634fe7c3"] [17592186382187 :answer-field/choices "answer-18320f2a-b614-5878-a30c-a6927276e203"] ["question-6a7e0438-dfaf-58ac-9ade-c318de994348" :question/id #uuid "6a7e0438-dfaf-58ac-9ade-c318de994348"] ["question-6a7e0438-dfaf-58ac-9ade-c318de994348" :question/math-academy-id "q-132637"] ["question-6a7e0438-dfaf-58ac-9ade-c318de994348" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{x}{y+z},$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists."] ["question-6a7e0438-dfaf-58ac-9ade-c318de994348" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when $y+z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y+z}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{(y+z)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+z)^{2} & \\ne 0 \\\\ y+z & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-6a7e0438-dfaf-58ac-9ade-c318de994348" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-115a4c5e-8438-57e0-8218-a23a116369b2" :answer-field/id #uuid "115a4c5e-8438-57e0-8218-a23a116369b2"] ["answer-bdb1c6fb-2082-5cdc-b6d2-731c9c2a61b8" :answer/id #uuid "bdb1c6fb-2082-5cdc-b6d2-731c9c2a61b8"] ["answer-bdb1c6fb-2082-5cdc-b6d2-731c9c2a61b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-bdb1c6fb-2082-5cdc-b6d2-731c9c2a61b8" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z<0\\}"] ["answer-365f8bbd-ce03-569d-aa3f-4ca177bcd8ce" :answer/id #uuid "365f8bbd-ce03-569d-aa3f-4ca177bcd8ce"] ["answer-365f8bbd-ce03-569d-aa3f-4ca177bcd8ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-365f8bbd-ce03-569d-aa3f-4ca177bcd8ce" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z>0\\,\\text{and}\\,x\\ne 0\\}"] ["answer-7240289b-9821-502d-a55b-8731f37e7d59" :answer/id #uuid "7240289b-9821-502d-a55b-8731f37e7d59"] ["answer-7240289b-9821-502d-a55b-8731f37e7d59" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-7240289b-9821-502d-a55b-8731f37e7d59" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z>0\\}"] ["answer-3d2420ba-4165-5c8f-9927-4bbc2b816817" :answer/id #uuid "3d2420ba-4165-5c8f-9927-4bbc2b816817"] ["answer-3d2420ba-4165-5c8f-9927-4bbc2b816817" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-3d2420ba-4165-5c8f-9927-4bbc2b816817" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x\\ne 0\\}"] ["answer-93c1ef46-0b11-5266-8f41-56facaa40905" :answer/id #uuid "93c1ef46-0b11-5266-8f41-56facaa40905"] ["answer-93c1ef46-0b11-5266-8f41-56facaa40905" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-93c1ef46-0b11-5266-8f41-56facaa40905" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y+z\\ne 0\\}"] ["answer-field-115a4c5e-8438-57e0-8218-a23a116369b2" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-115a4c5e-8438-57e0-8218-a23a116369b2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-115a4c5e-8438-57e0-8218-a23a116369b2" :answer-field/correct "answer-93c1ef46-0b11-5266-8f41-56facaa40905"] ["answer-field-115a4c5e-8438-57e0-8218-a23a116369b2" :answer-field/choices "answer-bdb1c6fb-2082-5cdc-b6d2-731c9c2a61b8"] ["answer-field-115a4c5e-8438-57e0-8218-a23a116369b2" :answer-field/choices "answer-365f8bbd-ce03-569d-aa3f-4ca177bcd8ce"] ["answer-field-115a4c5e-8438-57e0-8218-a23a116369b2" :answer-field/choices "answer-7240289b-9821-502d-a55b-8731f37e7d59"] ["answer-field-115a4c5e-8438-57e0-8218-a23a116369b2" :answer-field/choices "answer-3d2420ba-4165-5c8f-9927-4bbc2b816817"] ["answer-field-115a4c5e-8438-57e0-8218-a23a116369b2" :answer-field/choices "answer-93c1ef46-0b11-5266-8f41-56facaa40905"] ["question-6a7e0438-dfaf-58ac-9ade-c318de994348" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-115a4c5e-8438-57e0-8218-a23a116369b2"] [17592186124465 :knowledge-point/questions "question-6a7e0438-dfaf-58ac-9ade-c318de994348"] ["question-93b5347d-fa82-5e5e-b9aa-42f525c17d43" :question/id #uuid "93b5347d-fa82-5e5e-b9aa-42f525c17d43"] ["question-93b5347d-fa82-5e5e-b9aa-42f525c17d43" :question/math-academy-id "q-36525"] ["question-93b5347d-fa82-5e5e-b9aa-42f525c17d43" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-{x}^{2}}},$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ exists."] ["question-93b5347d-fa82-5e5e-b9aa-42f525c17d43" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ y & >{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-{x}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{-2x}{2(y-{x}^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{(y-{x}^{2})^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ y & >{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-93b5347d-fa82-5e5e-b9aa-42f525c17d43" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-d731db7f-90f6-50ca-af14-f65d325828c8" :answer-field/id #uuid "d731db7f-90f6-50ca-af14-f65d325828c8"] ["answer-14b28e62-5e5c-5fa4-828d-8176c468f073" :answer/id #uuid "14b28e62-5e5c-5fa4-828d-8176c468f073"] ["answer-14b28e62-5e5c-5fa4-828d-8176c468f073" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-14b28e62-5e5c-5fa4-828d-8176c468f073" :answer/value "\\{(x,y):y>{x}^{2}\\}"] ["answer-f1bbb24c-f728-542a-b6fd-83644bef18b4" :answer/id #uuid "f1bbb24c-f728-542a-b6fd-83644bef18b4"] ["answer-f1bbb24c-f728-542a-b6fd-83644bef18b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-f1bbb24c-f728-542a-b6fd-83644bef18b4" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y<{x}^{2}\\}"] ["answer-94fa791e-d489-52b8-9318-5172cd32b632" :answer/id #uuid "94fa791e-d489-52b8-9318-5172cd32b632"] ["answer-94fa791e-d489-52b8-9318-5172cd32b632" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-94fa791e-d489-52b8-9318-5172cd32b632" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ge {x}^{2}\\}"] ["answer-f3c96cca-e498-5185-8483-99a97501e744" :answer/id #uuid "f3c96cca-e498-5185-8483-99a97501e744"] ["answer-f3c96cca-e498-5185-8483-99a97501e744" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-f3c96cca-e498-5185-8483-99a97501e744" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\le {x}^{2}\\}"] ["answer-ac2ab564-0469-5d5f-bd5b-92ae71f9cfcb" :answer/id #uuid "ac2ab564-0469-5d5f-bd5b-92ae71f9cfcb"] ["answer-ac2ab564-0469-5d5f-bd5b-92ae71f9cfcb" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-ac2ab564-0469-5d5f-bd5b-92ae71f9cfcb" :answer/value "${f}_{x}$ exists everywhere on the $xy$-plane"] ["answer-field-d731db7f-90f6-50ca-af14-f65d325828c8" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-d731db7f-90f6-50ca-af14-f65d325828c8" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-d731db7f-90f6-50ca-af14-f65d325828c8" :answer-field/correct "answer-14b28e62-5e5c-5fa4-828d-8176c468f073"] ["answer-field-d731db7f-90f6-50ca-af14-f65d325828c8" :answer-field/choices "answer-14b28e62-5e5c-5fa4-828d-8176c468f073"] ["answer-field-d731db7f-90f6-50ca-af14-f65d325828c8" :answer-field/choices "answer-f1bbb24c-f728-542a-b6fd-83644bef18b4"] ["answer-field-d731db7f-90f6-50ca-af14-f65d325828c8" :answer-field/choices "answer-94fa791e-d489-52b8-9318-5172cd32b632"] ["answer-field-d731db7f-90f6-50ca-af14-f65d325828c8" :answer-field/choices "answer-f3c96cca-e498-5185-8483-99a97501e744"] ["answer-field-d731db7f-90f6-50ca-af14-f65d325828c8" :answer-field/choices "answer-ac2ab564-0469-5d5f-bd5b-92ae71f9cfcb"] ["question-93b5347d-fa82-5e5e-b9aa-42f525c17d43" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-d731db7f-90f6-50ca-af14-f65d325828c8"] [17592186124465 :knowledge-point/questions "question-93b5347d-fa82-5e5e-b9aa-42f525c17d43"] [17592186382200 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined when $-1\\le xy\\le 1.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le xy\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(xy)^{2}}}\\cdot x \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}{y}^{2} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{2} & <1 \\\\ -1<xy & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le xy\\le 1\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["answer-e48eb178-d3ae-5b7a-9e32-6647d4f89008" :answer/id #uuid "e48eb178-d3ae-5b7a-9e32-6647d4f89008"] ["answer-e48eb178-d3ae-5b7a-9e32-6647d4f89008" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-e48eb178-d3ae-5b7a-9e32-6647d4f89008" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1\\}"] ["answer-564c0717-2ab8-59c0-a054-e88b4b250ec9" :answer/id #uuid "564c0717-2ab8-59c0-a054-e88b4b250ec9"] ["answer-564c0717-2ab8-59c0-a054-e88b4b250ec9" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-564c0717-2ab8-59c0-a054-e88b4b250ec9" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le xy\\le 1\\}"] ["answer-6e63fc50-7cc5-5095-bd95-f14db7b98d09" :answer/id #uuid "6e63fc50-7cc5-5095-bd95-f14db7b98d09"] ["answer-6e63fc50-7cc5-5095-bd95-f14db7b98d09" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-6e63fc50-7cc5-5095-bd95-f14db7b98d09" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy<1\\}"] ["answer-79221a8e-d0bd-56c1-92e8-362d0c95c652" :answer/id #uuid "79221a8e-d0bd-56c1-92e8-362d0c95c652"] ["answer-79221a8e-d0bd-56c1-92e8-362d0c95c652" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-79221a8e-d0bd-56c1-92e8-362d0c95c652" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy>1\\}"] ["answer-856a2403-496c-5b21-9944-c9e1b1c13646" :answer/id #uuid "856a2403-496c-5b21-9944-c9e1b1c13646"] ["answer-856a2403-496c-5b21-9944-c9e1b1c13646" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-856a2403-496c-5b21-9944-c9e1b1c13646" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,xy\\ne 1\\}"] [17592186382201 :answer-field/correct "answer-e48eb178-d3ae-5b7a-9e32-6647d4f89008"] [17592186382201 :answer-field/choices "answer-e48eb178-d3ae-5b7a-9e32-6647d4f89008"] [17592186382201 :answer-field/choices "answer-564c0717-2ab8-59c0-a054-e88b4b250ec9"] [17592186382201 :answer-field/choices "answer-6e63fc50-7cc5-5095-bd95-f14db7b98d09"] [17592186382201 :answer-field/choices "answer-79221a8e-d0bd-56c1-92e8-362d0c95c652"] [17592186382201 :answer-field/choices "answer-856a2403-496c-5b21-9944-c9e1b1c13646"] [17592186339046 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y+z)$ is defined for any $x$, $y$, and $z.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}=\\frac{1}{1+(x+y+z)^{2}}$ is defined for any $x$, $y$, and $z.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domains of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["answer-d0ae369d-fbce-5a92-b83c-fdb84536b23d" :answer/id #uuid "d0ae369d-fbce-5a92-b83c-fdb84536b23d"] ["answer-d0ae369d-fbce-5a92-b83c-fdb84536b23d" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-d0ae369d-fbce-5a92-b83c-fdb84536b23d" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x+y+z)^{2}>1\\}"] ["answer-c7f3adcf-6cdf-592b-b898-6f8526012b19" :answer/id #uuid "c7f3adcf-6cdf-592b-b898-6f8526012b19"] ["answer-c7f3adcf-6cdf-592b-b898-6f8526012b19" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-c7f3adcf-6cdf-592b-b898-6f8526012b19" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z>-1\\}"] ["answer-cdbf817f-a650-51de-829b-812fa24d59e2" :answer/id #uuid "cdbf817f-a650-51de-829b-812fa24d59e2"] ["answer-cdbf817f-a650-51de-829b-812fa24d59e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-cdbf817f-a650-51de-829b-812fa24d59e2" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 0\\}"] ["answer-42768df1-45c5-5a14-8236-adb8b26820e4" :answer/id #uuid "42768df1-45c5-5a14-8236-adb8b26820e4"] ["answer-42768df1-45c5-5a14-8236-adb8b26820e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-42768df1-45c5-5a14-8236-adb8b26820e4" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x+y+z\\ne 1\\}"] ["answer-56dbc98e-986f-5c73-aa5f-cac881ecfa04" :answer/id #uuid "56dbc98e-986f-5c73-aa5f-cac881ecfa04"] ["answer-56dbc98e-986f-5c73-aa5f-cac881ecfa04" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-56dbc98e-986f-5c73-aa5f-cac881ecfa04" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\}"] [17592186338583 :answer-field/correct "answer-56dbc98e-986f-5c73-aa5f-cac881ecfa04"] [17592186338583 :answer-field/choices "answer-d0ae369d-fbce-5a92-b83c-fdb84536b23d"] [17592186338583 :answer-field/choices "answer-c7f3adcf-6cdf-592b-b898-6f8526012b19"] [17592186338583 :answer-field/choices "answer-cdbf817f-a650-51de-829b-812fa24d59e2"] [17592186338583 :answer-field/choices "answer-42768df1-45c5-5a14-8236-adb8b26820e4"] [17592186338583 :answer-field/choices "answer-56dbc98e-986f-5c73-aa5f-cac881ecfa04"] ["question-88858de7-2fd7-5431-aec4-cee489f567e8" :question/id #uuid "88858de7-2fd7-5431-aec4-cee489f567e8"] ["question-88858de7-2fd7-5431-aec4-cee489f567e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-72028"] ["question-88858de7-2fd7-5431-aec4-cee489f567e8" :question/problem "Given that $h(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{xz}{{y}^{2}})$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y}=-\\frac{2}{y},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{z},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $h$ exist."] ["question-88858de7-2fd7-5431-aec4-cee489f567e8" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $h(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{xz}{{y}^{2}})$ is defined only when $xz>0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $h$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{h}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${h}_{x}=\\frac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${h}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${h}_{y}=-\\frac{2}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${h}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{y}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${h}_{z}=\\frac{1}{z}$ is defined only when $z\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${h}_{z}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{h}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $h$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{h}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{h}_{z}} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\cap \\,\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-88858de7-2fd7-5431-aec4-cee489f567e8" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-13e96fd7-067f-5c7e-8c81-eec0b11e5b02" :answer-field/id #uuid "13e96fd7-067f-5c7e-8c81-eec0b11e5b02"] ["answer-c9035321-67de-5d87-8802-b231710205c2" :answer/id #uuid "c9035321-67de-5d87-8802-b231710205c2"] ["answer-c9035321-67de-5d87-8802-b231710205c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-c9035321-67de-5d87-8802-b231710205c2" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz>0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0\\}"] ["answer-6ae0216e-3dda-5b86-bf9a-7a2f1941c30d" :answer/id #uuid "6ae0216e-3dda-5b86-bf9a-7a2f1941c30d"] ["answer-6ae0216e-3dda-5b86-bf9a-7a2f1941c30d" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-6ae0216e-3dda-5b86-bf9a-7a2f1941c30d" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz\\ge 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x\\ne 0\\}"] ["answer-928531e1-339e-5d41-9d09-292c01dfe676" :answer/id #uuid "928531e1-339e-5d41-9d09-292c01dfe676"] ["answer-928531e1-339e-5d41-9d09-292c01dfe676" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-928531e1-339e-5d41-9d09-292c01dfe676" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xz\\le 0\\,\\text{and}\\,y>0\\}"] ["answer-a464505c-7c18-5824-af43-db6d78d3830b" :answer/id #uuid "a464505c-7c18-5824-af43-db6d78d3830b"] ["answer-a464505c-7c18-5824-af43-db6d78d3830b" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-a464505c-7c18-5824-af43-db6d78d3830b" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}"] ["answer-2ec0f707-e54c-51dd-acbe-ce56f922c04b" :answer/id #uuid "2ec0f707-e54c-51dd-acbe-ce56f922c04b"] ["answer-2ec0f707-e54c-51dd-acbe-ce56f922c04b" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-2ec0f707-e54c-51dd-acbe-ce56f922c04b" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z\\ne 0\\}"] ["answer-field-13e96fd7-067f-5c7e-8c81-eec0b11e5b02" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-13e96fd7-067f-5c7e-8c81-eec0b11e5b02" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-13e96fd7-067f-5c7e-8c81-eec0b11e5b02" :answer-field/correct "answer-c9035321-67de-5d87-8802-b231710205c2"] ["answer-field-13e96fd7-067f-5c7e-8c81-eec0b11e5b02" :answer-field/choices "answer-c9035321-67de-5d87-8802-b231710205c2"] ["answer-field-13e96fd7-067f-5c7e-8c81-eec0b11e5b02" :answer-field/choices "answer-6ae0216e-3dda-5b86-bf9a-7a2f1941c30d"] ["answer-field-13e96fd7-067f-5c7e-8c81-eec0b11e5b02" :answer-field/choices "answer-928531e1-339e-5d41-9d09-292c01dfe676"] ["answer-field-13e96fd7-067f-5c7e-8c81-eec0b11e5b02" :answer-field/choices "answer-a464505c-7c18-5824-af43-db6d78d3830b"] ["answer-field-13e96fd7-067f-5c7e-8c81-eec0b11e5b02" :answer-field/choices "answer-2ec0f707-e54c-51dd-acbe-ce56f922c04b"] ["question-88858de7-2fd7-5431-aec4-cee489f567e8" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-13e96fd7-067f-5c7e-8c81-eec0b11e5b02"] [17592186124557 :knowledge-point/questions "question-88858de7-2fd7-5431-aec4-cee489f567e8"] [17592186339047 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{1+y-{x}^{2}}}$ is defined only when $1+y-{x}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}=\\frac{xz}{\\sqrt{(1+y-{x}^{2})^{3}}}$ is defined only when $1+y-{x}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are the same\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["answer-58af975c-c2a1-526c-8913-b54cae912604" :answer/id #uuid "58af975c-c2a1-526c-8913-b54cae912604"] ["answer-58af975c-c2a1-526c-8913-b54cae912604" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-58af975c-c2a1-526c-8913-b54cae912604" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1\\}"] ["answer-8325a7fb-2d1d-57b2-90c3-eb44f3fb67db" :answer/id #uuid "8325a7fb-2d1d-57b2-90c3-eb44f3fb67db"] ["answer-8325a7fb-2d1d-57b2-90c3-eb44f3fb67db" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-8325a7fb-2d1d-57b2-90c3-eb44f3fb67db" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}>y+1\\}"] ["answer-41ff0dd7-d72c-5277-8d4d-17134cb43aa4" :answer/id #uuid "41ff0dd7-d72c-5277-8d4d-17134cb43aa4"] ["answer-41ff0dd7-d72c-5277-8d4d-17134cb43aa4" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-41ff0dd7-d72c-5277-8d4d-17134cb43aa4" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\ge y+1\\}"] ["answer-fcadf2f9-91d3-553d-9019-a4aa4c81b373" :answer/id #uuid "fcadf2f9-91d3-553d-9019-a4aa4c81b373"] ["answer-fcadf2f9-91d3-553d-9019-a4aa4c81b373" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-fcadf2f9-91d3-553d-9019-a4aa4c81b373" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y+1\\}"] ["answer-6c293847-d86d-5648-bc3a-b4c65524ca2d" :answer/id #uuid "6c293847-d86d-5648-bc3a-b4c65524ca2d"] ["answer-6c293847-d86d-5648-bc3a-b4c65524ca2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-6c293847-d86d-5648-bc3a-b4c65524ca2d" :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\ne y+1\\}"] [17592186338720 :answer-field/correct "answer-58af975c-c2a1-526c-8913-b54cae912604"] [17592186338720 :answer-field/choices "answer-58af975c-c2a1-526c-8913-b54cae912604"] [17592186338720 :answer-field/choices "answer-8325a7fb-2d1d-57b2-90c3-eb44f3fb67db"] [17592186338720 :answer-field/choices "answer-41ff0dd7-d72c-5277-8d4d-17134cb43aa4"] [17592186338720 :answer-field/choices "answer-fcadf2f9-91d3-553d-9019-a4aa4c81b373"] [17592186338720 :answer-field/choices "answer-6c293847-d86d-5648-bc3a-b4c65524ca2d"] [17592186339043 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $g(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["answer-783fd4c1-fb89-5517-bc35-b883098e9f40" :answer/id #uuid "783fd4c1-fb89-5517-bc35-b883098e9f40"] ["answer-783fd4c1-fb89-5517-bc35-b883098e9f40" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-783fd4c1-fb89-5517-bc35-b883098e9f40" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<0\\}"] ["answer-6e834be1-c403-5b49-87e3-229d8e48f719" :answer/id #uuid "6e834be1-c403-5b49-87e3-229d8e48f719"] ["answer-6e834be1-c403-5b49-87e3-229d8e48f719" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-6e834be1-c403-5b49-87e3-229d8e48f719" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\}"] ["answer-3d4cb978-08f2-56c9-a2ea-886a3b926325" :answer/id #uuid "3d4cb978-08f2-56c9-a2ea-886a3b926325"] ["answer-3d4cb978-08f2-56c9-a2ea-886a3b926325" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-3d4cb978-08f2-56c9-a2ea-886a3b926325" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0\\}"] ["answer-e4c394bb-40ee-5d34-8bd0-d21a98e21ea0" :answer/id #uuid "e4c394bb-40ee-5d34-8bd0-d21a98e21ea0"] ["answer-e4c394bb-40ee-5d34-8bd0-d21a98e21ea0" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-e4c394bb-40ee-5d34-8bd0-d21a98e21ea0" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\}"] ["answer-00e7e3fe-8aa5-5dbe-b351-f8ab041da26a" :answer/id #uuid "00e7e3fe-8aa5-5dbe-b351-f8ab041da26a"] ["answer-00e7e3fe-8aa5-5dbe-b351-f8ab041da26a" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-00e7e3fe-8aa5-5dbe-b351-f8ab041da26a" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\}"] [17592186338315 :answer-field/correct "answer-3d4cb978-08f2-56c9-a2ea-886a3b926325"] [17592186338315 :answer-field/choices "answer-783fd4c1-fb89-5517-bc35-b883098e9f40"] [17592186338315 :answer-field/choices "answer-6e834be1-c403-5b49-87e3-229d8e48f719"] [17592186338315 :answer-field/choices "answer-3d4cb978-08f2-56c9-a2ea-886a3b926325"] [17592186338315 :answer-field/choices "answer-e4c394bb-40ee-5d34-8bd0-d21a98e21ea0"] [17592186338315 :answer-field/choices "answer-00e7e3fe-8aa5-5dbe-b351-f8ab041da26a"] [17592186339044 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}\\,{e}^{x/y}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{(2x+y){e}^{x/y}}{2y\\sqrt{x}}$ is defined only when $x>0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{y}(x,y)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}{e}^{x/y}}{{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["answer-902fe96c-5b07-5372-abe9-ae12860196a2" :answer/id #uuid "902fe96c-5b07-5372-abe9-ae12860196a2"] ["answer-902fe96c-5b07-5372-abe9-ae12860196a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-902fe96c-5b07-5372-abe9-ae12860196a2" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x=0,y\\ne 0\\}"] ["answer-5a32d06b-cf7c-5422-99a6-39dd7a840412" :answer/id #uuid "5a32d06b-cf7c-5422-99a6-39dd7a840412"] ["answer-5a32d06b-cf7c-5422-99a6-39dd7a840412" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-5a32d06b-cf7c-5422-99a6-39dd7a840412" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y>0\\}"] ["answer-68c920e8-acc6-5f92-901b-f110118b4e1e" :answer/id #uuid "68c920e8-acc6-5f92-901b-f110118b4e1e"] ["answer-68c920e8-acc6-5f92-901b-f110118b4e1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-68c920e8-acc6-5f92-901b-f110118b4e1e" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0\\}"] ["answer-92d41990-0f98-5af9-a312-0df1d3c12535" :answer/id #uuid "92d41990-0f98-5af9-a312-0df1d3c12535"] ["answer-92d41990-0f98-5af9-a312-0df1d3c12535" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-92d41990-0f98-5af9-a312-0df1d3c12535" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y>0\\}"] ["answer-b712b312-6703-550f-a665-341165a0b1ea" :answer/id #uuid "b712b312-6703-550f-a665-341165a0b1ea"] ["answer-b712b312-6703-550f-a665-341165a0b1ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-b712b312-6703-550f-a665-341165a0b1ea" :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0\\}"] [17592186338495 :answer-field/correct "answer-68c920e8-acc6-5f92-901b-f110118b4e1e"] [17592186338495 :answer-field/choices "answer-902fe96c-5b07-5372-abe9-ae12860196a2"] [17592186338495 :answer-field/choices "answer-5a32d06b-cf7c-5422-99a6-39dd7a840412"] [17592186338495 :answer-field/choices "answer-68c920e8-acc6-5f92-901b-f110118b4e1e"] [17592186338495 :answer-field/choices "answer-92d41990-0f98-5af9-a312-0df1d3c12535"] [17592186338495 :answer-field/choices "answer-b712b312-6703-550f-a665-341165a0b1ea"] ["question-1ddf04e4-d463-5268-9d26-58b4ea7da263" :question/id #uuid "1ddf04e4-d463-5268-9d26-58b4ea7da263"] ["question-1ddf04e4-d463-5268-9d26-58b4ea7da263" :question/math-academy-id "q-72015"] ["question-1ddf04e4-d463-5268-9d26-58b4ea7da263" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)?$\n\nI. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(0,-1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{y}(1,1)$ is defined"] ["question-1ddf04e4-d463-5268-9d26-58b4ea7da263" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-y}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-y}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,-1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0-(-1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)=0,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(0,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,-1) & =\\frac{1}{0-(-1)}=\\frac{1}{1}=1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,-1) & =-\\frac{1}{0-(-1)}=-\\frac{1}{1}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0-1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1),\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0).\n$$\n\nwhich is also undefined. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] ["question-1ddf04e4-d463-5268-9d26-58b4ea7da263" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard] ["answer-field-7baa4b68-d188-53a1-8a4e-8080f4629446" :answer-field/id #uuid "7baa4b68-d188-53a1-8a4e-8080f4629446"] ["answer-e93c9a34-c0f4-5a44-8207-573fd788bf6b" :answer/id #uuid "e93c9a34-c0f4-5a44-8207-573fd788bf6b"] ["answer-e93c9a34-c0f4-5a44-8207-573fd788bf6b" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-e93c9a34-c0f4-5a44-8207-573fd788bf6b" :answer/value "I and II only"] ["answer-1f484384-2944-5aa5-be6d-f85e77de604a" :answer/id #uuid "1f484384-2944-5aa5-be6d-f85e77de604a"] ["answer-1f484384-2944-5aa5-be6d-f85e77de604a" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-1f484384-2944-5aa5-be6d-f85e77de604a" :answer/value "I only"] ["answer-895a6aba-7fbc-5c6f-b46f-27ec0e197bc2" :answer/id #uuid "895a6aba-7fbc-5c6f-b46f-27ec0e197bc2"] ["answer-895a6aba-7fbc-5c6f-b46f-27ec0e197bc2" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-895a6aba-7fbc-5c6f-b46f-27ec0e197bc2" :answer/value "II and III only"] ["answer-95fe521c-d73c-5a83-b40d-7b1ab28589f0" :answer/id #uuid "95fe521c-d73c-5a83-b40d-7b1ab28589f0"] ["answer-95fe521c-d73c-5a83-b40d-7b1ab28589f0" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-95fe521c-d73c-5a83-b40d-7b1ab28589f0" :answer/value "II only"] ["answer-8b5eecf6-bced-54a8-8975-13e55e4eb747" :answer/id #uuid "8b5eecf6-bced-54a8-8975-13e55e4eb747"] ["answer-8b5eecf6-bced-54a8-8975-13e55e4eb747" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-8b5eecf6-bced-54a8-8975-13e55e4eb747" :answer/value "III only"] ["answer-field-7baa4b68-d188-53a1-8a4e-8080f4629446" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-7baa4b68-d188-53a1-8a4e-8080f4629446" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-7baa4b68-d188-53a1-8a4e-8080f4629446" :answer-field/correct "answer-1f484384-2944-5aa5-be6d-f85e77de604a"] ["answer-field-7baa4b68-d188-53a1-8a4e-8080f4629446" :answer-field/choices "answer-e93c9a34-c0f4-5a44-8207-573fd788bf6b"] ["answer-field-7baa4b68-d188-53a1-8a4e-8080f4629446" :answer-field/choices "answer-1f484384-2944-5aa5-be6d-f85e77de604a"] ["answer-field-7baa4b68-d188-53a1-8a4e-8080f4629446" :answer-field/choices "answer-895a6aba-7fbc-5c6f-b46f-27ec0e197bc2"] ["answer-field-7baa4b68-d188-53a1-8a4e-8080f4629446" :answer-field/choices "answer-95fe521c-d73c-5a83-b40d-7b1ab28589f0"] ["answer-field-7baa4b68-d188-53a1-8a4e-8080f4629446" :answer-field/choices "answer-8b5eecf6-bced-54a8-8975-13e55e4eb747"] ["question-1ddf04e4-d463-5268-9d26-58b4ea7da263" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-7baa4b68-d188-53a1-8a4e-8080f4629446"] [17592186124466 :knowledge-point/questions "question-1ddf04e4-d463-5268-9d26-58b4ea7da263"] ["question-73a5b476-e534-54e3-9da7-2de0545b138d" :question/id #uuid "73a5b476-e534-54e3-9da7-2de0545b138d"] ["question-73a5b476-e534-54e3-9da7-2de0545b138d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72017"] ["question-73a5b476-e534-54e3-9da7-2de0545b138d" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}?$\n\nI. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(2,-1)$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(1,0)$ is defined\n\nIII. ${f}_{y}(0,1)$ is defined"] ["question-73a5b476-e534-54e3-9da7-2de0545b138d" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(2,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(2,-1)=\\frac{2}{(-1)}=-2,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,-1) & =\\frac{1}{-1}=-1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(2,-1) & =-\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\frac{1}{0}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is true. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1)=\\frac{0}{1}=0,\n$$\n\n  and  ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{y}(0,1)=\\frac{-0}{{1}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."] ["question-73a5b476-e534-54e3-9da7-2de0545b138d" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-68e03a66-dcc9-5dc9-9fd3-bdca1b4cd4cd" :answer-field/id #uuid "68e03a66-dcc9-5dc9-9fd3-bdca1b4cd4cd"] ["answer-edfadd89-b8d6-5bb0-9938-41414b42c7a0" :answer/id #uuid "edfadd89-b8d6-5bb0-9938-41414b42c7a0"] ["answer-edfadd89-b8d6-5bb0-9938-41414b42c7a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-edfadd89-b8d6-5bb0-9938-41414b42c7a0" :answer/value "I and III only"] ["answer-2641419a-4016-5b04-ae0d-2222660b4187" :answer/id #uuid "2641419a-4016-5b04-ae0d-2222660b4187"] ["answer-2641419a-4016-5b04-ae0d-2222660b4187" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-2641419a-4016-5b04-ae0d-2222660b4187" :answer/value "I only"] ["answer-198bc42c-d3a2-5588-8d98-878c5a5390fe" :answer/id #uuid "198bc42c-d3a2-5588-8d98-878c5a5390fe"] ["answer-198bc42c-d3a2-5588-8d98-878c5a5390fe" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-198bc42c-d3a2-5588-8d98-878c5a5390fe" :answer/value "I, II, and III"] ["answer-576eba6e-2ba8-56c8-a2cb-7dfa5b572111" :answer/id #uuid "576eba6e-2ba8-56c8-a2cb-7dfa5b572111"] ["answer-576eba6e-2ba8-56c8-a2cb-7dfa5b572111" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-576eba6e-2ba8-56c8-a2cb-7dfa5b572111" :answer/value "II only"] ["answer-51e3c066-4569-5d03-9a91-b2ea8579daae" :answer/id #uuid "51e3c066-4569-5d03-9a91-b2ea8579daae"] ["answer-51e3c066-4569-5d03-9a91-b2ea8579daae" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-51e3c066-4569-5d03-9a91-b2ea8579daae" :answer/value "III only"] ["answer-field-68e03a66-dcc9-5dc9-9fd3-bdca1b4cd4cd" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-68e03a66-dcc9-5dc9-9fd3-bdca1b4cd4cd" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-68e03a66-dcc9-5dc9-9fd3-bdca1b4cd4cd" :answer-field/correct "answer-edfadd89-b8d6-5bb0-9938-41414b42c7a0"] ["answer-field-68e03a66-dcc9-5dc9-9fd3-bdca1b4cd4cd" :answer-field/choices "answer-edfadd89-b8d6-5bb0-9938-41414b42c7a0"] ["answer-field-68e03a66-dcc9-5dc9-9fd3-bdca1b4cd4cd" :answer-field/choices "answer-2641419a-4016-5b04-ae0d-2222660b4187"] ["answer-field-68e03a66-dcc9-5dc9-9fd3-bdca1b4cd4cd" :answer-field/choices "answer-198bc42c-d3a2-5588-8d98-878c5a5390fe"] ["answer-field-68e03a66-dcc9-5dc9-9fd3-bdca1b4cd4cd" :answer-field/choices "answer-576eba6e-2ba8-56c8-a2cb-7dfa5b572111"] ["answer-field-68e03a66-dcc9-5dc9-9fd3-bdca1b4cd4cd" :answer-field/choices "answer-51e3c066-4569-5d03-9a91-b2ea8579daae"] ["question-73a5b476-e534-54e3-9da7-2de0545b138d" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-68e03a66-dcc9-5dc9-9fd3-bdca1b4cd4cd"] [17592186124466 :knowledge-point/questions "question-73a5b476-e534-54e3-9da7-2de0545b138d"]] "retractions" [[17592186382186 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & \\ge 0\\,\\text{and}\\,y\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y\\ge 0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{\\sqrt{x+y}}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}-\\sqrt{x+y}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y-2x-2y}{2{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x+y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2x-y}{2{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x+y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y\\ge 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,x+y>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186382187 :answer-field/correct 17592186382190] [17592186382187 :answer-field/choices 17592186382192] [17592186382187 :answer-field/choices 17592186382189] [17592186382187 :answer-field/choices 17592186382190] [17592186382187 :answer-field/choices 17592186382191] [17592186382200 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined when $-1\\le xy\\le 1.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le xy\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(xy)^{2}}}\\cdot x \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}{y}^{2} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{2} & <1 \\\\ -1<xy & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le xy\\le 1}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186382201 :answer-field/correct 17592186382204] [17592186382201 :answer-field/choices 17592186382202] [17592186382201 :answer-field/choices 17592186382203] [17592186382201 :answer-field/choices 17592186382204] [17592186382201 :answer-field/choices 17592186382205] [17592186382201 :answer-field/choices 17592186382206] [17592186339046 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y+z)$ is defined for any $x$, $y$, and $z.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}=\\frac{1}{1+(x+y+z)^{2}}$ is defined for any $x$, $y$, and $z.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domains of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338583 :answer-field/correct 17592186336445] [17592186338583 :answer-field/choices 17592186336081] [17592186338583 :answer-field/choices 17592186337405] [17592186338583 :answer-field/choices 17592186337387] [17592186338583 :answer-field/choices 17592186336460] [17592186338583 :answer-field/choices 17592186336445] [17592186339047 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{1+y-{x}^{2}}}$ is defined only when $1+y-{x}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}=\\frac{xz}{\\sqrt{(1+y-{x}^{2})^{3}}}$ is defined only when $1+y-{x}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are the same\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}<y+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338720 :answer-field/correct 17592186338218] [17592186338720 :answer-field/choices 17592186338218] [17592186338720 :answer-field/choices 17592186336612] [17592186338720 :answer-field/choices 17592186336871] [17592186338720 :answer-field/choices 17592186336089] [17592186338720 :answer-field/choices 17592186337482] [17592186339043 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $g(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338315 :answer-field/correct 17592186337292] [17592186338315 :answer-field/choices 17592186337921] [17592186338315 :answer-field/choices 17592186337875] [17592186338315 :answer-field/choices 17592186337575] [17592186338315 :answer-field/choices 17592186337292] [17592186338315 :answer-field/choices 17592186337566] [17592186339044 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}\\,{e}^{x/y}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{(2x+y){e}^{x/y}}{2y\\sqrt{x}}$ is defined only when $x>0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{y}(x,y)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}{e}^{x/y}}{{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338495 :answer-field/correct 17592186337416] [17592186338495 :answer-field/choices 17592186337698] [17592186338495 :answer-field/choices 17592186337476] [17592186338495 :answer-field/choices 17592186337416] [17592186338495 :answer-field/choices 17592186337337] [17592186338495 :answer-field/choices 17592186336555]]}]